From: Project Luma <luma@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: copy embedded Tokay diagnostic DTB before unflatten

The accepted diagnostic linked its FDT in .init.rodata, so unflattened
property values were overwritten with POISON_FREE_INITMEM after PID 1 began.
The attempted move to persistent .rodata changed the early image layout and
did not produce a physical native-execution marker.

Keep the physically accepted init-section placement.  Linux already provides
unflatten_and_copy_device_tree() specifically for an FDT built into kernel
init memory.  Select that path only for the explicit Luma Tokay diagnostic;
the disabled configuration retains the normal arm64 firmware-FDT behavior.

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/setup.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/setup.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/setup.c
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/setup.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/setup.c
@@ -338,8 +338,12 @@ void __init __no_sanitize_address setup_arch(char **cmdline_p)
 	/* Parse the ACPI tables for possible boot-time configuration */
 	acpi_boot_table_init();
 
-	if (acpi_disabled)
+	if (acpi_disabled) {
+		if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_LUMA_TOKAY_EMBEDDED_DIAGNOSTIC))
+			unflatten_and_copy_device_tree();
+		else
+			unflatten_device_tree();
+	}
-		unflatten_device_tree();
 
 	bootmem_init();
 
-- 
